build/
esp32/build/
esp32/sdkconfig
esp32/sdkconfig.old
esp32/managed_components/
# esp32/dependencies.lock is deliberately NOT ignored. It pins the exact versions the
# component manager resolved, and firmware built from the same commit should contain the
# same code — "~1.2.0" would otherwise let two boards flashed a week apart carry
# different provisioning stacks, which is not something the version string on the fleet
# page could tell you.
esp32/main/secrets.h
# The per-role variants too. The pair is built from one source tree with two different
# configurations — secrets.ble.h defines the master, secrets.wifi.h adds
# CIELOTRACK_ROLE_WIFI and CIELOTRACK_SENSOR_ONLY — and one is copied over secrets.h to
# choose which image is built.
#
# They exist because editing secrets.h in place is what silently dropped the peer
# configuration from a master build once, compiling out the Wi-Fi forwarding path while
# the master went on reporting itself perfectly healthy. Keeping both files means the
# choice is a copy rather than an edit.
#
# Ignored for the obvious reason: they carry the same device keys and network credentials
# secrets.h does.
esp32/main/secrets.*.h
